  weekTwoToast:"Eight in the morning. Doña Chelo is unlocking the gate on the southwest lot — and leaving it open. Nothing behind you closes either.",
  mepi1:"Doña Chelo counts the drawer twice, the way she has for thirty years, and this time the number matches what the screen said all week. “You didn't build me a robot,” she says. “You built me a Tuesday I can trust.” Frijol sleeps on the scale. Outside, somebody is laying track. Before you're out the door she's on the landline. “Tacho. He told me to stop paying him for something. Send him your worst.”",
  mepi2:"Some of it stuck. Some of it Nando quietly went back to doing by hand, and nobody made a thing of it. Doña Chelo shrugs. “Half of it works. That's more than the last guy.” Your name is on the wall by the register now, beside the permit, under a name in older handwriting you still do not recognize — and nobody is going to tell you whose it is today. On the landline, later: “Tacho. Half of what he did works. That's more than the last one.”",
  mepi3:"The mercado survived you. Most of the list is still a list, and the pilot is a folder nobody opens — but Doña Chelo hands you a bag of pan dulce anyway. “Come back when you've thought about it.” The door does not lock behind you. A week later she phones Tacho and says one thing: “He's honest about what he doesn't know. Start there.”",
